export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-2","number":2,"title":"Sources","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Define the category of sound generators in electronic synthesis and identify the oscillator as the primary generator. - Manipulate pitch parameters with precision across Octave, Semitone (100 cents), and Fine (cents) domains.","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-2-1-1","code":"2.1","title":"The Oscillator","blurb":"- Define the category of sound generators in electronic synthesis and identify the oscillator as the primary generator. - Manipulate pitch parameters with precision across Octave, Semitone (100 cents), and Fine (cents) domains.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-4","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define the category of <strong>sound generators</strong> in electronic synthesis and identify the oscillator as the primary generator.","Manipulate pitch parameters with precision across <strong>Octave</strong>, <strong>Semitone</strong> (100 cents), and <strong>Fine</strong> (cents) domains.","Distinguish pre-filter oscillator level (drive / mix stage) from post-filter amplifier gain.","Decode analog hardware terminology (<strong>VCO</strong>, <strong>DCO</strong>, <strong>NCO</strong>) and translate it to digital software parameters.","Locate and navigate the primary oscillator sections across all studio engines: <code>Drift</code>, <code>Analog</code>, <code>Operator</code>, <code>Vital</code>, and <code>Surge XT</code>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-1","title":"2.1.1 — Sound Generators: The Category","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.1 — Sound Generators: The Category","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In sound synthesis, a <strong>generator</strong> is any functional module that originates an audio signal from zero state (as opposed to a processor, which merely modifies an existing signal)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            SYNTHESIS SOUND GENERATORS                         |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| GENERATOR TYPE       | OPERATIONAL MECHANISM                                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Oscillators**      | Generates continuous, periodic mathematical waveforms  |\n|                      | (Sine, Saw, Square, Triangle, Wavetable frames).       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Noise Generators** | Generates non-periodic, random or pseudo-random voltage|\n|                      | fluctuations across the frequency spectrum.            |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Samplers**         | Plays back pre-recorded PCM audio buffers from RAM/disk|\n|                      | (`Simpler`, `Drum Sampler`).                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Physical Models**  | Computes mathematical differential equations simulating|\n|                      | physical vibrating structures (`Tension`, `Collision`).|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Every subtractive patch begins at the generator stage. If the raw generator output does not contain the required harmonic foundation, no subsequent filter or effect can synthesize those missing frequencies out of thin air."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.1 (Sound Generators)","<em>PML Synthesis – 1 – Oscillator</em>, Chapter 1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-2","title":"2.1.2 — Oscillator as the Usual Generator","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.2 — Oscillator as the Usual Generator","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>oscillator</strong> is the foundational generator in subtractive, FM, and wavetable synthesizers. It is an electronic or algorithmic circuit that outputs a recurring voltage pattern cycling at an audio-rate frequency (20 Hz - 20 kHz)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ PITCH INPUT (MIDI Note) ] ===&gt; [ OSCILLATOR CORE ] ===&gt; [ PERIODIC AUDIO WAVEFORM ]\n                                        |\n                            Frequency = f (Cycles / Sec)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When the oscillator cycles at 110 Hz, the human ear hears the pitch A_1.","When the oscillator cycles at 440 Hz, the human ear hears the pitch A_3.","The geometric profile of the cycle (the wave shape) dictates how many harmonics accompany that fundamental pitch."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.2 (The Oscillator Unit)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-3","title":"2.1.3 — Shape, Octave, Semitone, Fine (Cents)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.3 — Shape, Octave, Semitone, Fine (Cents)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tuning an oscillator requires absolute command of four standard pitch and timbre controls found on every synthesizer interface:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| Control          | Step Resolution       | Acoustic / Musical Effect          |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Wave Shape**   | Continuous / Discrete | Selects the harmonic distribution  |\n|                  | (Sine, Saw, Pulse...) | (Timbre).                          |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Octave (Foot)**| Multiples of 2x / 0.5x| Shifts pitch by $\\pm 12\\text{ st}$ |\n|                  | (e.g., -2, -1, 0, +1) | ($32', 16', 8', 4', 2'$ pipe feet).|\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Semitone / Coarse**| $1\\text{ semitone}$| Shifts pitch by chromatic steps    |\n|                  | ($\\pm 1 \\text{ to } 48\\text{ st}$)| (Keys / Intervals: +7 = 5th).     |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+\n| **Fine (Cents)** | $1\\text{ cent}$       | Micro-tuning adjustments           |\n|                  | ($1/100\\text{th of a semitone}$)| Creates phase beating / detune.  |\n+------------------+-----------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Tuning Math for Designers","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["1 Octave = 12 Semitones = 1200 Cents = Frequency × 2.","Sub-bass layering typically requires dropping Oscillator 2 by -1 Octave (-12 semitones).","Detuning two identical sawtooth oscillators by +7 cents and -7 cents causes acoustic phase interference, creating the rhythmic beating that forms the foundation of supersaws and Reese basses."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 1 – Oscillator</em>, Chapter 2 (Pitch Architecture)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1 &amp; §5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-4","title":"2.1.4 — Level Before the Filter vs. Amp After","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.4 — Level Before the Filter vs. Amp After","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A crucial gain staging concept in synthesizer architecture is understanding the difference between <strong>Oscillator Level (Mixer Stage)</strong> and <strong>Amplifier Gain (VCA Stage)</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSC 1 ] --(Osc 1 Level)--&gt; [ MIXER / DRIVE ] ===&gt; [ FILTER ] ===&gt; [ VCA / AMP ] ===&gt; OUTPUT\n[ OSC 2 ] --(Osc 2 Level)--&gt;        |                                    |\n                                    v                                    v\n                           Pre-Filter Saturation                Master Output Level</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Oscillator Level (Pre-Filter):</strong> Controls the amplitude of the signal entering the filter circuit. In analog-modeled synths (<code>Drift</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>, <code>Analog</code>), driving oscillator levels to maximum (100%) drives the virtual filter input into non-linear saturation and harmonic distortion.","<strong>Amplifier Gain (Post-Filter):</strong> Controls the clean final volume of the shaped sound before track output. It does not introduce saturation into the filter stage."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Engineering Rule:</strong> If your low-pass filter sounds too aggressive or refuses to mute high frequencies cleanly, lower the individual oscillator levels in the mixer to prevent pre-filter circuit clipping."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.3 (Mixer vs. VCA Stages)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift / Analog Signal Routing)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-5","title":"2.1.5 — VCO Language (Decoding Voltage Terminology)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.5 — VCO Language (Decoding Voltage Terminology)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Many digital software instruments use historical analog synthesizer nomenclature:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| Analog Terminology                 | Digital Software Translation             |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **VCO** (Voltage Controlled Osc)   | The digital oscillator module.           |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **DCO** (Digitally Controlled Osc) | Analog circuits clocked by digital timers|\n|                                    | (perfect tuning stability).              |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **Footage ($32', 16', 8', 4', 2'$)**| Octave selectors derived from pipe organs:|\n|                                    | $8' = \\text{Standard Pitch (C3)}$,       |\n|                                    | $16' = -1\\text{ Octave}$, $32' = -2\\text{ Oct}$|\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **CV (Control Voltage)**           | Modulation source signals (LFO/Envelope).|\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"When you see \"8'\" in <code>Analog</code> or a vintage emulation, understand that it represents standard un-transposed pitch; \"16'\" drops the oscillator into bass territory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2 &amp; §15 (Glossary)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Architecture)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-1-6","title":"2.1.6 — Finding the Oscillator Section Across Studio Engines","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.6 — Finding the Oscillator Section Across Studio Engines","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To achieve engine independence, you must instantly locate the oscillator core across all installed instruments without searching through menus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Engine        | Oscillator Section Navigation                                         |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **`Drift`**   | Leftmost panel: **Osc 1** (Shape, Oct, Mod) and **Osc 2** (Shape, Semi,|\n|               | Detune, Level).                                                       |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **`Analog`**  | Top-left quadrant: Click **Osc 1** or **Osc 2** to display Shape,     |\n|               | Octave, Semi, Detune, Sub/Sync in the central display.                |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **`Operator`**| Four bottom-left color-coded blocks: **A, B, C, D**. Click block **A** |\n|               | to access Waveform, Coarse/Fine ratios, and Additive Partial editor.  |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **`Vital`**   | Top row: **OSC 1, OSC 2, OSC 3**. Contains 3D Wavetable display,     |\n|               | Pitch/Semitone transpositions, Spectral Warp knob, and Unison count.  |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **`Surge XT`**| Top left of Scene panel: **Oscillator 1, 2, 3**. Click dropdown to    |\n|               | select algorithm (Classic, Modern, Wavetable, FM2/FM3...).           |\n+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Scavenger Drill:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Open Ableton Live. Load <code>Drift</code>, <code>Analog</code>, <code>Operator</code>, <code>Vital</code>, and <code>Surge XT</code> on five adjacent tracks.","Step through each track and configure Oscillator 1 to a pure <strong>Sawtooth</strong> wave tuned down by <strong>-1 Octave</strong>.","Confirm that you can execute this operation in under 15 seconds across all five engines."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-2-2-2","code":"2.2","title":"The Four Waveforms (and Pulse)","blurb":"- Identify and characterize the four classical geometric waveforms (Sine, Triangle, Square, Sawtooth) by ear, mathematical harmonic series, and spectrum profile. - Formulate the geometric rule of thumb: sharper geometric transitions generat","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-5","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Identify and characterize the four classical geometric waveforms (<strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Triangle</strong>, <strong>Square</strong>, <strong>Sawtooth</strong>) by ear, mathematical harmonic series, and spectrum profile.","Formulate the geometric rule of thumb: <strong>sharper geometric transitions generate higher harmonic density</strong>.","Manipulate <strong>Pulse Width (PW)</strong> and program <strong>Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)</strong> via LFO for rich chorus-like movement on single oscillators.","Execute blind waveform identification drills using real-time spectrum analysis as verification."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-1","title":"2.2.1 — Sine: Pure Fundamental","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.1 — Sine: Pure Fundamental","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Sine Wave</strong> is the foundational building block of all audio acoustics (Fourier Theorem: all complex signals can be decomposed into a sum of sine waves)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude\n  1.0 |      ***\n      |    *     *\n  0.0 |--*---------*---------*---&gt; Time\n      |              *     *\n -1.0 |                ***</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Spectrum:\nAmplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |  | [Fundamental f_0 Only]\n       |  |\n-60 dB +--+------------------------&gt; Frequency (Hz)\n         f_0</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Harmonic Composition:</strong> Contains <strong>only the fundamental frequency (f_0)</strong>; zero overtones or upper partials (n = 1).","<strong>Subtractive Utility:</strong> Poor as a raw subtractive source. Since it has no upper harmonics, placing a low-pass filter on a sine wave does nothing except attenuate the total volume of the note.","<strong>Primary Roles:</strong> Clean sub-bass foundations (30-60 Hz), pure organ drawbars, 808 kick drum bodies, and carrier/modulator signals in FM synthesis (<code>Operator</code>)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.4 (Sine Wave Acoustics)","<em>PML Synthesis – 2 – Waveforms</em>, Chapter 1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-2","title":"2.2.2 — Triangle: Soft Odd Harmonics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.2 — Triangle: Soft Odd Harmonics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Triangle Wave</strong> is a mellow, warm waveform characterized by a linear rise and fall."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude\n  1.0 |      /\\\n      |     /  \\\n  0.0 |----+----+----+----&gt; Time\n      |   /      \\\n -1.0 |  /        \\</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Harmonic Falloff: A_n = 1 / n^2 (Odd Harmonics Only)\nSpectrum:\nAmplitude\n  1.0 |  | (f_0)\n      |\n  0.1 |        | (3f_0, Amp = 1/9)\n 0.04 |              | (5f_0, Amp = 1/25)\n      +--+-----+-----+-----&gt; Frequency\n        f_0   3f_0  5f_0</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Harmonic Composition:</strong> Contains <strong>only odd integer harmonics</strong> (1f_0, 3f_0, 5f_0, 7f_0,).","<strong>Mathematical Amplitude Rule:</strong> Harmonic amplitudes fall off rapidly at the inverse square of the harmonic index:"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"A_n = 1n^2 (1, 19, 125, 149, )"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Timbral Character:</strong> Mellow, soft, slightly wooden, flute-like.","<strong>Primary Roles:</strong> Warm sub-bass with gentle speaker presence, mellow leads, soft synth keys, flute emulations."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.5 (Triangle Wave)","<em>PML Synthesis – 2 – Waveforms</em>, Chapter 2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-3","title":"2.2.3 — Square: Hollow Odd Harmonics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.3 — Square: Hollow Odd Harmonics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Square Wave</strong> is an abrupt, binary waveform that switches instantaneously between positive and negative peak values with a 50% duty cycle."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude\n  1.0 |  +-----+\n      |  |     |\n  0.0 |--+-----+--+----+----&gt; Time\n      |        |  |\n -1.0 |        +--+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Harmonic Falloff: A_n = 1 / n (Odd Harmonics Only)\nSpectrum:\nAmplitude\n  1.0 |  | (f_0)\n      |\n  0.3 |        | (3f_0, Amp = 1/3)\n  0.2 |              | (5f_0, Amp = 1/5)\n  0.1 |                    | (7f_0, Amp = 1/7)\n      +--+-----+-----+-----+-----&gt; Frequency\n        f_0   3f_0  5f_0  7f_0</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Harmonic Composition:</strong> Contains <strong>only odd integer harmonics</strong> (1f_0, 3f_0, 5f_0, 7f_0,).","<strong>Mathematical Amplitude Rule:</strong> Harmonic amplitudes fall off gradually at the inverse of the harmonic index:"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"A_n = 1n (1, 13, 15, 17, )"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Timbral Character:</strong> Hollow, woody, nasal, clarinet-like, vintage chiptune/8-bit character.","<strong>Primary Roles:</strong> Classic house/garage organ stabs, hollow low-end basses, aggressive acid leads, clarinet/reed instruments."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.6 (Square Wave)","<em>PML Synthesis – 2 – Waveforms</em>, Chapter 3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-4","title":"2.2.4 — Sawtooth: Full Harmonic Spectrum","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.4 — Sawtooth: Full Harmonic Spectrum","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Sawtooth Wave</strong> (Saw) is the most harmonically dense and versatile waveform in subtractive synthesis. It features a linear ramp followed by an instantaneous vertical reset."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude\n  1.0 |    /|    /|    /|\n      |   / |   / |   / |\n  0.0 |--+--+--+--+--+--+--&gt; Time\n      | /   | /   | /   |\n -1.0 |/    |/    |/    |</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Harmonic Falloff: A_n = 1 / n (All Integer Harmonics: Even + Odd)\nSpectrum:\nAmplitude\n  1.0 |  | (f_0)\n  0.5 |     | (2f_0, Amp = 1/2)\n  0.3 |        | (3f_0, Amp = 1/3)\n  0.2 |           | (4f_0, Amp = 1/4)\n  0.2 |              | (5f_0, Amp = 1/5)\n      +--+--+--+--+--+-----&gt; Frequency\n        f_0 2f 3f 4f 5f</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Harmonic Composition:</strong> Contains <strong>all integer harmonics</strong> (both even and odd: 1f_0, 2f_0, 3f_0, 4f_0, 5f_0, 6f_0,).","<strong>Mathematical Amplitude Rule:</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"A_n = 1n (1, 12, 13, 14, 15, )"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Timbral Character:</strong> Bright, buzzy, sharp, brassy.","<strong>Subtractive Clay:</strong> Because the sawtooth wave contains every harmonic across the audible spectrum, it serves as the ultimate starting material for subtractive filtering."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.7 (Sawtooth Wave)","<em>PML Synthesis – 2 – Waveforms</em>, Chapter 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-5","title":"2.2.5 — Spectrogram Lab: Why Shapes Sound Different","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.5 — Spectrogram Lab: Why Shapes Sound Different","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                              CLASSICAL WAVEFORM COMPARISON                              |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+\n| Waveform  | Harmonics Present    | Amplitude Decay Rate      | Acoustic Character       |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+\n| **Sine**  | Fundamental Only     | None (0 overtones)        | Pure, smooth, round      |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+\n| **Triangle**| Odd Harmonics Only | Rapid ($\\frac{1}{n^2}$)   | Soft, muted buzz         |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+\n| **Square**| Odd Harmonics Only   | Gradual ($\\frac{1}{n}$)   | Hollow, woody, nasal     |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+\n| **Saw**   | All Harmonics (Even+Odd)| Gradual ($\\frac{1}{n}$)| Bright, buzzy, brassy    |\n+-----------+----------------------+---------------------------+--------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Geometric Rule of Sound Design","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sharper Geometric Discontinuities Denser High-Frequency Partial Energy"},{"type":"list","items":["A smooth sine wave has zero sharp corners  zero overtones.","A triangle wave has angled peaks  mild upper overtones.","A square wave has instantaneous vertical leaps  prominent odd overtones.","A sawtooth wave has a sharp point <em>and</em> an instantaneous vertical reset  all overtones present."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.8 (Waveform Geometry)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-6","title":"2.2.6 — Pulse Width as a Fifth Practical Control","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.6 — Pulse Width as a Fifth Practical Control","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Pulse Wave</strong> is an asymmetrical variation of the square wave where the ratio between the positive peak duration and negative trough duration—known as the <strong>Duty Cycle</strong>—is adjustable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       50% Duty Cycle (Square)                  25% Duty Cycle (Narrow Pulse)\n  +1.0 |  +-----+                          +1.0 |  +--+\n       |  |     |                               |  |  |\n   0.0 |--+-----+--+----+----&gt; Time         0.0 |--+--+-----+----+----&gt; Time\n       |        |  |                            |     |     |\n  -1.0 |        +--+                       -1.0 |     +-----+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When Duty Cycle = 50%, the wave is a symmetric Square wave (pure odd harmonics).","When Duty Cycle shifts away from 50% (e.g., 25%, 10%, or 75%), <strong>even harmonics are introduced into the spectrum</strong>.","As the pulse becomes narrower (&lt;10%), the sound becomes increasingly thin, reedy, and nasal."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.9 (Pulse Width)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-7","title":"2.2.7 — PWM: LFO → Width (Alive Pads and Leads)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.7 — PWM: LFO → Width (Alive Pads and Leads)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)</strong> is the continuous, automated modulation of a pulse wave's duty cycle using an LFO."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ LFO (0.5 Hz Sine) ] ===(Modulation Amount)===&gt; [ OSC 1 Pulse Width ]\n                                                        |\n                                                        v\n                                         [ Continuously Shifting Harmonics ]\n                                                        |\n                                                        v\n                                         [ Chorus / Ensemble Phasing Effect ]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Acoustic Mechanism","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"As the duty cycle shifts cyclically from 10% to 90%, the amplitudes of individual even and odd harmonics rise and fall out of phase with one another. This creates complex internal phase cancellations that mimic the sound of <strong>two slightly detuned oscillators playing simultaneously</strong>, while consuming the CPU of only a single voice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It (Classic PWM Pad in <code>Drift</code>):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Load an initialized <code>Drift</code>.","Set Osc 1 Shape to <strong>Pulse</strong> (the rectangle icon).","In Drift's modulation section, route <strong>LFO</strong> to <code>Osc 1 PW</code> (Pulse Width).","Set LFO Shape to <strong>Sine/Triangle</strong>, Rate to 0.4 Hz, and Depth to 0.60.","Play a sustained chord (C Maj_7). Observe the rich, shimmering, animate movement generated by a single oscillator without using any chorus audio effect."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.10 (PWM Architecture)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift Modulation Matrix)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-2-8","title":"2.2.8 — Ear Quiz Prep: Blind Waveform Identification","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.8 — Ear Quiz Prep: Blind Waveform Identification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To pass <strong>Gate A</strong>, you must be able to identify any classical waveform by ear instantly without looking at the synth GUI."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Ear Identification Decision Tree:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Is the sound completely pure with zero buzz or edge?</strong> → <strong>SINE WAVE</strong>","<strong>Is the sound bright, harsh, buzzy, and brass-like?</strong> → <strong>SAWTOOTH WAVE</strong>","<strong>Is the sound hollow, woody, or reminiscent of an old Nintendo console or clarinet?</strong> → <strong>SQUARE WAVE</strong>","<strong>Is the sound soft and warm, but with a subtle muffled buzz behind the fundamental?</strong> → <strong>TRIANGLE WAVE</strong>","<strong>Is the sound reedy, thin, and nasal like a harpsichord or mosquito?</strong> → <strong>NARROW PULSE WAVE</strong>"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.11 (Ear Training Protocol)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1 &amp; §15"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-2-3-3","code":"2.3","title":"Noise and Other Generators","blurb":"- Define noise as non-periodic stochastic energy containing all frequencies simultaneously with no perceived musical fundamental pitch. - Compare and differentiate White Noise (equal energy per Hertz) and Pink Noise (equal energy per octave","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-6","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define <strong>noise</strong> as non-periodic stochastic energy containing all frequencies simultaneously with no perceived musical fundamental pitch.","Compare and differentiate <strong>White Noise</strong> (equal energy per Hertz) and <strong>Pink Noise</strong> (equal energy per octave / -3 dB/oct rolloff).","Deploy noise generators as layering tools to inject attack transients, breath, and high-frequency \"air\" into tonal sounds.","Utilize noise as the primary generator for percussion design (snares, hi-hats, claps) and atmospheric risers/sweeps.","Complete the <strong>Gate A Self-Assessment Checkpoint</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-1","title":"2.3.1 — What Noise Is (Random Energy, No Fundamental Pitch)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.1 — What Noise Is (Random Energy, No Fundamental Pitch)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While classical oscillators generate periodic (repeating) waveforms that produce a recognizable musical pitch, a <strong>noise generator</strong> produces a completely non-periodic, stochastic signal."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Oscillator (Periodic -&gt; Distinct Pitch):\n  1.0 |   /\\    /\\    /\\    /\\\n  0.0 |--+--+--+--+--+--+--+--+--&gt; Time (Repeats every period T)\n -1.0 | /    \\/    \\/    \\/    \\\n\nNoise Generator (Non-Periodic / Stochastic -&gt; No Pitch):\n  1.0 |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n  0.0 |--+-++-++-+++-++-+-++-+---&gt; Time (Completely random fluctuations)\n -1.0 | | || || || || || || ||</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Spectrum of Pure Noise:\nAmplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | ==================================== [Continuous flat energy across all frequencies]\n-60 dB +------------------------------------&gt; Frequency (20 Hz - 20 kHz)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Because noise contains continuous energy across all frequencies simultaneously without an integer-harmonic structure, the human ear cannot detect a fundamental frequency or assign it a musical note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.12 (Noise Fundamentals)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-2","title":"2.3.2 — White vs. Pink vs. Other Colors","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.2 — White vs. Pink vs. Other Colors","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Noise is classified into standard acoustic \"colors\" based on its power spectral density (how acoustic energy is distributed across the frequency spectrum):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| Noise Color      | Spectral Energy Distribution     | Timbral Character &amp; Sound Design Role   |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **White Noise**  | Equal energy per **Hertz**       | Bright, harsh, hissing (like a television|\n|                  | ($0\\text{ dB/oct}$ slope).       | static). Best for sharp hi-hats, claps, |\n|                  | Energy doubles each octave.      | snare snaps, and bright risers.         |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **Pink Noise**   | Equal energy per **Octave**      | Balanced, warm, natural (like heavy rain|\n|                  | ($-3\\text{ dB/oct}$ rolloff).    | or a waterfall). Best for warm acoustic |\n|                  | Matches human ear perception.    | breath, natural wind, and snare bodies. |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+\n| **Brownian / Red**| Heavy low-frequency bias        | Deep, dark, rumbling (like distant      |\n|                  | ($-6\\text{ dB/oct}$ rolloff).    | thunder). Best for ocean surf, seismic  |\n|                  |                                  | rumbles, and deep atmospheric pads.     |\n+------------------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Energy Profiles:\n  dB | [White Noise: Flat 0 dB/oct]\n     | ------------------------------------\n     | [Pink Noise: -3 dB/oct]\n     | \\\n     |   \\\n     |     \\\n     |       \\ [Brownian Noise: -6 dB/oct]\n     +------------------------------------&gt; Frequency</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.13 (Spectral Noise Colors)","<em>PML Synthesis – 1 – Oscillator</em>, Chapter 3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-3","title":"2.3.3 — Noise as Attack Transient / Air / Breath","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.3 — Noise as Attack Transient / Air / Breath","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Noise is rarely used purely on its own in melodic instruments; instead, it is blended into tonal patches to provide acoustic realism and physical presence."}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Attack Transient Injection (Snappy Plucks & Leads)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When shaping an acoustic pluck (e.g., a plucked string or thumb slap), the initial impact of the physical pick or plectrum contains a micro-burst of inharmonic noise (10-30 ms)."},{"type":"list","items":["Layer a short burst of noise with an instant decay (20 ms) over a sustained sawtooth bass or lead to give the note onset a sharp, physical \"click\" that cuts through dense arrangements."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. High-Frequency Air and Breath","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Flutes, brass instruments, and human vocal cords all generate subtle turbulent air friction."},{"type":"list","items":["Mixing in a subtle level (-24 dBFS) of high-passed pink or white noise above 6 kHz into a lush synth pad adds immediate depth, organic texture, and analog presence."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.14 (Transient &amp; Air Layering)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-4","title":"2.3.4 — Noise as Primary Sound Source: Percussion and FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.4 — Noise as Primary Sound Source: Percussion and FX","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Because noise provides a dense, continuous frequency canvas, subtractive filtering can sculpt it into complete percussion instruments and transitional sound effects without using standard oscillators."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ WHITE NOISE GENERATOR ]\n           |\n           v\n[ BAND-PASS FILTER (1.5 kHz) ] ===&gt; Removes unwanted sub and harsh top end\n           |\n           v\n[ ENVELOPE (Attack 0ms, Decay 180ms, Sustain 0dB) ] ===&gt; Shapes percussive envelope\n           |\n           v\n[ OUTPUT: SYNTHETIC SNARE / CLAP BODY ]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Primary Sound Recipes Derived from Noise:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Hi-Hats:</strong> White noise → High-Pass Filter (8 kHz) → Ultra-short decay envelope (30 ms).","<strong>Snare Wire Layer:</strong> White noise → Band-Pass Filter (2-4 kHz) → Medium decay envelope (150 ms).","<strong>Noise Riser / Sweep:</strong> White noise → Low-Pass Filter → Automate Filter Cutoff sweeping from 100 Hz to 18 kHz over 8 or 16 bars with high resonance."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 2.15 (Noise Percussion Design)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12 (Recipe 4)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-5","title":"2.3.5 — Wavetable and Sample-Based Sources (Preview)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.5 — Wavetable and Sample-Based Sources (Preview)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While subtractive synthesis begins with geometric waveforms and noise, modern sound design expands generator capabilities into two advanced domains:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Wavetable Generators (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>):</strong> Instead of looping a single geometric cycle, wavetable synths store a sequence of single-cycle waveform \"frames\" in an array (a wavetable). By modulating the <strong>Wavetable Position</strong>, the oscillator smoothly interpolates between different harmonic profiles over time before any filtering is applied.","<strong>Sample-Based Generators (<code>Simpler</code>, <code>Drum Sampler</code>):</strong> Plays recorded audio files directly. A recorded sample can be processed through the exact same subtractive signal path (Filter → ADSR → LFO) as a synthetic oscillator."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>(These generator architectures are explored in full depth in Module 9 and Module 10).</em>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7 &amp; §12 (Recipe E)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-2-3-6","title":"2.3.6 — Gate A: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.6 — Gate A: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to <strong>Module 3 (Filters and the Frequency Map)</strong>, verify your mastery of Module 1 and Module 2 competencies."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             GATE A CHECKLIST                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ ] 1. Waveform Ear Identification:                                           |\n|     Play Sine, Triangle, Square, and Sawtooth blindly at C3. Can you identify |\n|     all four accurately with 100% reliability?                                |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 2. Harmonic Math Explanation:                                             |\n|     Can you articulate in plain language why a Sawtooth wave sounds brighter  |\n|     than a Square wave (All harmonics at 1/n vs. Odd harmonics only)?         |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 3. Generator Literacy:                                                    |\n|     Can you explain why placing a low-pass filter on a pure sine wave only    |\n|     reduces volume rather than changing its tone color?                       |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 4. PWM Operation:                                                         |\n|     Can you manually configure an LFO to modulate the Pulse Width of a square |\n|     wave in Drift or Surge XT to create a chorus-like pad?                    |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 5. Noise Application:                                                     |\n|     Can you construct a clean synthetic closed hi-hat from scratch using      |\n|     only white noise, a high-pass filter, and an ADSR envelope?               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If all 5 criteria are verified by ear and manual execution, <strong>Gate A is passed</strong>. Proceed to <strong>Module 3</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §3 (Gate A Definition)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
